                <pb facs="1050" n="956" sameAs="frus:frus1887/pg956"/>

                <head>No. 589.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Bayard to Mr. Lothrop.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1887-04-08"><hi rendition="#italic">April</hi> 8,
                        1887</date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 81.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have received your No. 103 touching a
                    proposed modification of the Russian law on the subject of naturalization. A
                    movement in this direction is very gratifying. You will omit no discreet
                    endeavor to encourage the disposition and lead it towards a fuller recognition
                    of the inherent right of expatriation, which is now firmly rooted in
                    international law.</p>
                <p>I am, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">T. F. Bayard.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
